  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Finding the Total Differential of a Function Defined in Terms of Two Variables</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Finding the total differential.</p>
<p>Let \(z ={@f_xy@} \). Find \(dz\)</p>
<br>We proceed in 2 steps.
<br><b>Step 1: </b> We compute the partial derivatives: \(f_{x}\) and \(f_{x}\) such that \( z = f(x, y) \).
<br>\(f_{x}=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]
 <br>\(f_{y}=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]
<br><b>Step 2: </b> Deduce the total differential of \(z\). 
<br>\(dz=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked solution</b></p>
<p>Finding the total differential.</p>
<p><b>Recall (Definition): </b> Let \(z =f(x, y)\) be continuous on an open set \(S\). Let \(dx\) and \(dy\) represent changes in \(x\) and \(y\), respectively. </p>
<p>Where the partial derivatives \(f_{x}\) and \(f_{y}\) exist, the total differential of \(z\) is</p>
<p> \[ dz = f_{x} (x, y) \cdot dx + f_{y} (x, y) \cdot dy \] </p>
<p>Let \(z ={@f_xy@} \). Find \(dz\)</p>
<p>We proceed in 2 steps.</p>
<p>Step 1:  We compute the partial derivatives: \(f_{x}\) and \(f_{y}\) such that \( z = f(x, y) \).</p>
<p>\( 
\begin{align}
f_{x} & = & \frac{\partial }{\partial x} \left( {@f_xy@} \right) \\
 & = & {@ta_1@}\\
\end{align}
\)
</p>
 <and>
<p>\( 
\begin{align}
f_{y} & = & \frac{\partial }{\partial y} \left( {@f_xy@} \right) \\
 & = & {@ta_2@}\\
\end{align}
\)
</p>
<p>Step 2:  Deduce the total differential of \(z\).</p> 
\(dz={@ta_1@} \cdot dx+{@ta_2@} \cdot dy \);]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
